  "query_preview": "..\nActivity thus far has been confined to northern \nGraham/Greenlee, with a couple transient supercells already \nnoted. These initial cells should continue to push NW and out \nof the CWA within the next hour or so. \n\nFor the rest of the day...Looking at the current MesoAnalysis \nshows MLCAPE 1000-2000 j/kg with pockets slightly over 2000 j/kg\nin southern Cochise County. Effective shear is modest around \n20-35 kts, which supports another favorable environment for \nstrong to severe multicells and one or two transient supercells.\nCAMs also show this scenario, but coverage would be isolated at\nbest. Main threats will be wind gusts to 60 mph and marginally \nsevere hail to around 1\" in diameter. While heavy rain is \npossible, flash flooding would be very localized given the \nprogressive motion of any storms. \n\nTomorrow is tricky forecast, as there are hints of another \nlarge Sonoran MCS which could skirt or sneak into extreme \nsouthern AZ. While confidence is low at this time, its worth \nmentioning the possibility. This would increase a localized \nsevere/flash flood risk along the International Border. The\nflash flooding risk would be greatest for areas saturated in \nthe San Pedro watershed.\n\nNo changes to the Extreme Heat Warning as heat will build the \nrest of week, peaking this weekend. NAEFS mean 500 mb heights at\nthe 99.5 percentile or max compared to the CFSR climatology \nThursday into the weekend. Temperatures warm 7-12 degrees above \nnormal during this time frame leading to widespread areas of \nMajor to Extreme HeatRisk. Because of this an Extreme Heat \nWarning remains is in place Thursday through Sunday. The two \nwarmest days look to be Friday and Saturday with Tucson possibly\nseeing 110 degrees both days. NBM probabilities for Tucson \nInternational to hit 110 degrees have dropped into the 20-35%\nrange both days, but still quite signfciant. \n\nStorm chances drop to their lowest of the week Thursday, before\nslowly ramping back up by the weekend. Greatest coverage \nremains south and east of Tucson through the rest of the week, \nwith some day to day variability based on the previous days \nconvection.",
